OPEN–CLOSED PRINCIPLE (OCP)

Definition:
----------------
Software entities (classes, modules, functions)
should be OPEN for extension but CLOSED for modification.

Meaning:
----------------
• You should be able to add new behavior
  without changing existing, tested code.
• Achieved using:
  - Interfaces
  - Abstract classes
  - Polymorphism

Why OCP is Important:
----------------
• Prevents breaking existing functionality
• Reduces regression bugs
• Improves maintainability
• Encourages clean architecture

Bad Design (Violates OCP):
----------------
• if-else or when chains based on type
• Frequent modification of core classes
• Tight coupling

Good Design (Follows OCP):
----------------
• Depend on abstractions
• Use polymorphism
• Add new classes instead of modifying old ones

Real-World Analogy:
----------------
Switch Board:
• Old way: Modify wiring for every new appliance ❌
• OCP way: Plug new appliance into socket ✅

Android Usage Examples:
----------------
• RecyclerView.Adapter
• ViewModel with interfaces
